## 15. Task.Run + WaitAll

```csharp
void Process()
{
    Task task1 =
        Task.Run(() => Work1());

    Task task2 =
        Task.Run(() => Work2());

    Task.WaitAll(task1, task2);
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
                Main/Current Thread
                       |
                       |
                  Task.Run()
                   /     \
                  /       \
                 v         v
          ThreadPool 1   ThreadPool 2
             |               |
           Work1()          Work2()
             |               |
             +-------+-------+
                     |
                     v
                  complete
```

Meanwhile:

```text
Current Thread
      |
      v
  WaitAll()
      |
      v
   BLOCKED
```

The current thread is not doing `Work1` or `Work2`.

It is simply blocked waiting for them.

---

## 16. Task.Run + await

Compare:

```csharp
async Task ProcessAsync()
{
    Task task1 =
        Task.Run(() => Work1());

    Task task2 =
        Task.Run(() => Work2());

    await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2);
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
Current Thread
      |
      +---- Task.Run()
      |
      +---- Task.Run()
      |
      +---- await WhenAll
      |
      +---- Thread is FREE
```

```text
ThreadPool Thread 1
      |
      +---- Work1()
```

```text
ThreadPool Thread 2
      |
      +---- Work2()
```

Both complete:

```text
Both complete
      |
      v
ProcessAsync resumes
```

Key difference:

```text
WaitAll
    |
    v
Current thread BLOCKED
```

```text
await WhenAll
    |
    v
Current thread NOT BLOCKED
```

---

## 17. DO WE ALWAYS NEED Task.Run?

No.

For CPU-bound synchronous work:

```csharp
int Calculate()
{
    // expensive CPU work
}
```

You may use:

```csharp
await Task.Run(() => Calculate());
```

when you want to offload the CPU work to the ThreadPool.

But for native asynchronous I/O:

```csharp
await database.GetCustomerAsync();
```

you generally do **NOT** need:

```csharp
await Task.Run(() =>
    database.GetCustomerAsync());
```

The database API is already asynchronous.

---

## 18. CPU-BOUND EXAMPLE

```csharp
int Calculate()
{
    int sum = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000_000; i++)
    {
        sum += i;
    }

    return sum;
}
```

To offload this work:

```csharp
async Task<int> CalculateAsync()
{
    return await Task.Run(() => Calculate());
}
```

Caller:

```csharp
int result = await CalculateAsync();
```

Flow:

```text
CalculateAsync()
       |
       v
   Task.Run()
       |
       v
   ThreadPool
       |
       v
   Calculate()
       |
       v
   CPU work
       |
       v
   Task completes
       |
       v
   await resumes
```

---

## 19. I/O-BOUND EXAMPLE

Suppose the database provider has:

```csharp
Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync();
```

Use:

```csharp
async Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync()
{
    return await database.GetCustomerAsync();
}
```

Not normally:

```csharp
async Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync()
{
    return await Task.Run(() =>
        database.GetCustomerAsync());
}
```

Why?

Because the database API already provides asynchronous I/O.

The goal is:

```text
Application Thread
       |
       v
Start database I/O
       |
       v
     await
       |
       v
Thread becomes available
```

```text
Database
       |
       v
  Performs I/O
       |
       v
  Operation completes
       |
       v
  Task completes
       |
       v
  Method resumes
```

No application thread needs to sit blocked waiting for the database.

---

## 20. ASYNC ALL THE WAY

Suppose:

```text
Controller
    |
    v
Service
    |
    v
Repository
    |
    v
Database
```

Database:

```csharp
Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync();
```

Repository:

```csharp
async Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync()
{
    return await database.GetCustomerAsync();
}
```

Service:

```csharp
async Task<Customer> GetCustomerAsync()
{
    return await repository.GetCustomerAsync();
}
```

Controller:

```csharp
async Task<IActionResult> GetCustomer()
{
    Customer customer =
        await service.GetCustomerAsync();

    return Ok(customer);
}
```

The Task propagates upward:

```text
Database
    |
    | Task<Customer>
    v
Repository
    |
    | Task<Customer>
    v
Service
    |
    | Task<Customer>
    v
Controller
```

This is commonly described as:

> "async all the way."

It does **NOT** mean every method in an application must be async.

It means:

> "Don't unnecessarily convert asynchronous waiting into synchronous blocking."

---

## 21. FINAL MENTAL MODEL

Memorize this matrix:

|  | ONE TASK | MANY TASKS |
|---|---|---|
| **BLOCKING** | `task.Wait()` → **BLOCKS thread** | `Task.WaitAll()` → **BLOCKS thread** |
| **NON-BLOCKING** | `await task` → **DOES NOT BLOCK thread** | `await Task.WhenAll()` → **DOES NOT BLOCK thread** |

### ONE TASK

```text
Wait()
    = Block
```

```text
await
    = Don't block
```

### MANY TASKS

```text
WaitAll()
    = Block
```

```text
WhenAll()
    = Combined Task
```

```text
await WhenAll()
    = Don't block
```

