## 22. INTERVIEW ANSWER

### Question

> "What is the difference between Wait, await, WaitAll, and WhenAll?"

### Answer

> "`Task.Wait()` synchronously blocks the current thread until one Task completes, while `await` asynchronously waits for one Task without blocking the current thread.
>
> `Task.WaitAll()` synchronously blocks until multiple Tasks complete.
>
> `Task.WhenAll()` creates a Task representing the completion of multiple Tasks, which I can asynchronously await using `await Task.WhenAll(...)`.
>
> Therefore, in asynchronous application code, I generally prefer `await` and `Task.WhenAll` over `Wait` and `WaitAll` because they avoid blocking threads."

